## Whether to trust an answer now

At every fidelity interval ({{ fidelity.interval }}) the twin checks itself against the real system:{% if fidelity.scored %}whether its forecasts came true within their tolerance over the last {{ fidelity.window }}, {% endif %}whether its data is as fresh as agreed, and whether its sensors still behave. What it found is one state: `fidelity` in `/meta/health` (with `fidelity_detail`: since when, the last check, how many incidents are open and failed, how many signals are suspect), and in detail at `GET /fidelity`.

| State | What it means | With an answer to a question of the contract |
|---|---|---|
| `unknown` | no check has completed yet | give the answer and say that the twin has not been checked against the real system yet |
| `healthy` | the last check found everything within what was agreed | give the answer |
| `correcting` | an incident is open and the twin is putting it right | give the answer, say that the twin is correcting and name the incident if it concerns this question (`GET /fidelity`, `incidents`); do not base a decision or an action on it |
| `failed` | the twin could not put itself right | report the answer as unreliable, in the same sentence as the answer, whatever it is; do not base a decision or an action on it; tell the user the twin needs attention |

- Read the state at the start of the session and again before any answer a decision will rest on: it can change at every check.
- An answer of a twin that is `failed` is never presented as the twin's reliable answer, not even when it looks plausible.
- How each measure is computed, with this twin's settings, and where to read it: `reference/fidelity.md`.

## Incidents: what the twin does by itself

Each thing that goes wrong is one incident (`GET /fidelity/incidents?status=unresolved`; `GET /fidelity/incidents/{id}` lists every step taken). The twin works on them itself, at every check; you do not need to start anything:

{% if fidelity.scored %}
- `breach`: a forecast question whose error over the window is above its tolerance. The twin re-fits the active model on recent data (up to {{ fidelity.recalibrate_attempts }} times){% if fidelity.reselect %}, then makes the best of the specification's candidates active{% endif %}{% if fidelity.agent_remodel %}, then asks the managing agent to revise the model (status `remodel_requested`){% endif %}. After {{ fidelity.retry_budget }} rounds without success the incident is `failed`.
{% endif %}
{% if fidelity.stale %}
- `stale`: a question whose data is older than agreed. It resolves when fresh data arrives, and fails after {{ fidelity.retry_budget }} stale checks in a row.
{% endif %}
- `signal`: a sensor whose readings look stuck or biased. Sync stops writing its value{% if fidelity.scored %}, the twin writes an inferred one where a forecast question covers it,{% endif %} and the raw readings are kept on the signal (`GET /fidelity/signals`). It clears itself after 3 checks with plausible readings.
{% if executes %}
- `reconcile`: an executed action whose effect was not observed within its settle time. It resolves when a later action on the same control and object is observed to take effect.
{% endif %}

What is yours to do:

{% if remodel %}
- An incident in status `remodel_requested`, or the notification `twin.fidelity.remodel_requested`: follow `reference/remodel.md`. The twin waits {{ fidelity.remodel_timeout }} for a revised model, then the incident is `failed`.
{% endif %}
- A `failed` incident: tell the user what failed and since when. `POST /fidelity/incidents/{id}/retry` starts its corrections again; send it only when the user says so.
- A `signal`{% if executes %} or `reconcile`{% endif %} incident whose cause the user says is mended (a sensor replaced, a reading checked on site): `POST /fidelity/signals/{uid}/clear`{% if executes %} or `POST /fidelity/incidents/{id}/resolve`{% endif %}, with `by` (the user) and `note` (what was done). Only on the user's word.
- Never retry, resolve or clear anything to make the state look better: the state is what the user relies on.
- The twin tells its owner of each change of state and incident {% if notification == "webhook" %}by a webhook{% else %}in its log{% endif %}; `GET /lifecycle/notifications` lists the last 200 since its API started.

{% endif %}
